<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Usman dan Fodio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1754">1754</a>–<a href="/years/1817">1817</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as dan Fodio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Usman dan Fodio was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> Islamic scholar and reformer who led a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> kingdoms beginning in 1804 and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>. His movement created one of the largest states in nineteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and inspired other reform movements across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. The jihad also threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a>, contributing to its decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio.jpg" alt="Usman dan Fodio" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anasskoko · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Usman_Ibn_Fodio_Calligraphy_02.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Usman was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a> on 15 December 1754 into the Toronkawa, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> scholarly clan whose ancestors had migrated from Futa Tooro in the 1300s and settled in Hausaland. His family moved to Degel, where he studied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> with his father before moving to other teachers. At the age of 20 he set up his own school at Degel. From 1774 he travelled as an itinerant preacher, for twelve years in Gobir and Kebbi and then five years in Zamfara. His students included his younger brother Abdullahi and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunfa">Yunfa</a>.</p><p>In his writings he condemned the ruling elite for enslavement, idol worship, overtaxation, arbitrary rule and greed, and he insisted on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a> of law. Peasants and other poorer people followed him in large numbers, as did <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> pastoralists whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> officials had seized.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1802 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunfa">Yunfa</a>, once his student, turned against him, revoked the autonomy of Degel and tried to have him killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alkalawa">Alkalawa</a>. Usman wrote the Masa'il muhimma on the obligation to emigrate from persecution, and his community fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudu">Gudu</a>, a journey of four or five days. There his followers elected him Commander of the Believers, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullahi-dan-fodio">Abdullahi dan Fodio</a> and Muhammed Bello the first to pledge allegiance. He was about fifty, took no part in the fighting and left the military command to others.</p><p>The Muslims defeated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a> force at Tabkin Kwotto and, after the harvest of October 1804, began an aggressive campaign in the hinterland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alkalawa">Alkalawa</a>. After the defeat of Gobir, many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> kings attacked the Muslim communities in their lands, and Usman appointed fourteen leaders and sent them back to their territories to lead <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>. He passed the actual leadership of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> state to his son Muhammed Bello and moved to Sokoto in 1815.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Usman died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> on 20 April 1817, aged 62. Muhammed Bello succeeded him as second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, and his brother Abdullahi became emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwandu-emirate">Gwandu</a>. By 1830 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> had engulfed most of what is now northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>, and until the British conquest at the beginning of the 20th century there were twelve caliphs. He had promoted education for women as well as men, and his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nana-asmau">Nana Asma'u</a> translated some of his works into local languages. Muslims view him as a mujaddid, a renewer of the faith. His example inspired later jihads, including those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seku-amadu">Seku Amadu</a> and Omar Saidou Tall.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Usman dan Fodio” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Gobir the preacher <b>Usman dan Fodio</b> built a large following that demanded strict application of Islamic law, the abolition of non-Islamic taxes and an end to the enslavement of Muslims.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausa kingdoms · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The nineteenth century brought acute crisis with the Fulani jihad inspired by <b>Usman dan Fodio</b>: Fulani forces sacked Ngazargamu in 1808.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom was also a centre of Islamic learning: scholars came from Mali, Bornu and North Africa, and al-Maghili’s advice to Rumfa became a reference point for Islamic government in West Africa, quoted three centuries later in a work attributed to the Fulani reformer <b>Usman dan Fodio</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-kano">Kingdom of Kano · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The last independent Sultan, Attahiru I, answered the British conquest with a hijra eastwards that deliberately echoed <b>dan Fodio</b>'s founding migration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 18th century the Tuareg scholar Jibril ibn ’Umar preached revolutionary jihad, and his student <b>Usman dan Fodio</b> went on to lead the Sokoto jihads and found the Sokoto Caliphate.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tuareg">Tuareg · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The movement belonged to the revivalist trends of the 18th and 19th centuries, including the Mahdist movement in Sudan, the Senussi in Libya, the Fulani movement of Uthman <b>Dan Fodio</b>, the Faraizi movement in Bengal, Sayyid Ahmed Barelvi's Mujahidin and the Padri movement in Indonesia.</blockquote><a href="/movement/wahhabism">Wahhabism · Background</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Maratta, in what is now <a href="/countries/niger">Niger</a>. Died in Sokoto, in what is now <a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.29,5.53&amp;year=1794" aria-label="Maratta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Usman dan Fodio"><image href="/maps/globes/g00000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5458 3791h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5458 3791h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.29,5.53&amp;year=1794">What was here in 1794? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
